  "worked_solution": "We find the reciprocal of the base $(4),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{-3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1}{4\\times 4\\times 4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
